`bin/karr` starts with `#!/usr/bin/env perl`, and ExtUtils::MakeMaker installs
that line unchanged (it only rewrites a shebang that names perl directly). On
Windows with Strawberry Perl the script lands in `C:\Strawberry\perl\site\bin`;
from Git Bash `env perl` is `/usr/bin/perl`, the stripped MSYS perl that ships
with Git for Windows and has no App::karr:

    $ karr list --compact
    Can't locate App/karr/SyncGuard.pm in @INC (... /usr/lib/perl5/site_perl ...)
      at /c/Strawberry/perl/site/bin/karr line 8.
    $ /c/Strawberry/perl/bin/perl /c/Strawberry/perl/site/bin/karr board     -> works

`karr.bat` (pl2bat, `perl -x -S`) picks the same wrong perl there. The Bash
tool of Claude Code on Windows is Git Bash, so every agent that follows the
karr skills and types `karr ...` fails on its first command; the message
names a missing module of karr's own, which reads like a broken install.
(windows-developer hub, docs/karr-windows.md, B7; hit again 2026-10-07 by an
agent in another repository.)

Options, to be measured on Windows:
1. `#!perl` in the source, so MakeMaker writes the installing perl's absolute
   path -- check that MSYS bash executes a shebang with a Windows path, and
   what that means for the house default of `#!/usr/bin/env perl`.
2. A `karr.exe` (PAR::Packer or portable Strawberry plus launcher) as a
   release asset; `scripts/build-binary.sh` and release-binaries.yml build
   linux-x86_64 and linux-aarch64 only.
3. Documentation plus a shim (`exec /c/Strawberry/perl/bin/perl .../karr "$@"`)
   as the stopgap -- the windows-developer hub carries one as `tools/karr`.

Done when `karr --version` answers from PowerShell, cmd and Git Bash after
the documented Windows install, and README says how.

