2026-10-07 17:10 Measured on Windows 11, Strawberry Perl 5.42.0, ExtUtils::MakeMaker 7.76, Git for Windows bash (windows-developer k19):

Option 1 (`#!perl`) does not work. On Win32 `$Config{startperl}` is `#!perl`, so MakeMaker's fixin has no absolute path to write: a throwaway dist with three EXE_FILES came out of `perl Makefile.PL && gmake` with the first lines unchanged -- `#!perl`, `#!/usr/bin/env perl`, `#!/usr/bin/perl` -- and all three, started from Git Bash, ran under `/usr/bin/perl` (MSYS, v5.42.2). The generated .bat is `perl -x -S %0 %*`, PATH-dependent as before.

What Git Bash does execute is an absolute path to Strawberry in the shebang, in every spelling: `#!/c/Strawberry/perl/bin/perl`, `#!C:/Strawberry/perl/bin/perl.exe` and `#!C:\Strawberry\perl\bin\perl.exe` each gave `$^X = C:\Strawberry\perl\bin\perl.exe`. So a shebang fix would have to be written by karr's own install step (or by hand after each `cpanm App::karr`), not by MakeMaker.

Option 3 as measured: a starter `~/bin/karr` (`exec /c/Strawberry/perl/bin/perl /c/Strawberry/perl/site/bin/karr "$@"`) wins, because `~/bin` is the first PATH entry of the Claude Code Bash tool, ahead of the Strawberry directories. `karr --version` and `karr list --compact` now answer from Git Bash in a foreign repo on this machine. PowerShell and cmd never had the problem (karr.bat finds Strawberry's perl there).

Still open: Getty's choice between an install-time shebang rewrite, a karr.exe, or documenting the starter.

